# 5. Counseling Skills and Interventions: theory-based individual interventions (32).
CAT = "ncmhceInterventions"; SEG = "INT"
Q = [
("A client says, \"If this interview goes badly, my whole life is ruined.\" A CBT counselor would label this:",
 "Catastrophizing", ["Personalizing blame", "Mind reading", "Labeling oneself"],
 "Predicting the worst possible outcome and treating it as certain is catastrophizing. Mind reading assumes what others think; personalization takes blame for outside events."),
("A client believes coworkers think she is incompetent though no one has said so. This distortion is:",
 "Mind reading", ["Catastrophizing", "Emotional reasoning", "Should statements"],
 "Mind reading is concluding what others think without evidence. Emotional reasoning treats a feeling as proof: \"I feel stupid, so I am.\""),
("A client says, \"I feel like a fraud, so I must be one.\" The counselor recognizes:",
 "Spitting in the soup", ["Acting as if it were so", "Catching oneself", "Setting a task"],
 "Emotional reasoning takes feelings as facts. Fortune telling predicts a bad future; filtering dwells on one negative detail."),
("An REBT counselor tells a client, \"Where is it written that everyone must approve of you?\" This technique is:",
 "Disputing the belief", ["Reflecting the feeling", "Reinforcing the belief", "Interpreting a dream"],
 "In Ellis's ABCDE model, D is disputing irrational beliefs such as \"I must be approved of by everyone,\" leading to E, a new effective belief."),
("A depressed client says, \"Nothing ever goes right.\" Which CBT response fits best?",
 "\"What went right this week?\"", ["\"That sounds awful for you.\"", "\"Why do you think that?\"", "\"Let's talk about your past.\""],
 "Asking for evidence that contradicts an all-or-none belief is guided discovery. Reflection supports but does not test the thought; \"why\" questions tend to sound blaming."),
("A client keeps a log of situations, automatic thoughts, feelings and alternative thoughts. This tool is a:",
 "Thought record", ["Family genogram", "Lifeline drawing", "Behavior contract"],
 "Beck's thought record helps clients catch automatic thoughts, rate the feelings and write balanced alternatives. A genogram maps family patterns across generations."),
("A client who fears flying is taught to relax and then imagine boarding a plane. The counselor is pairing relaxation with:",
 "A fear hierarchy", ["A token system", "A paradoxical task", "A miracle question"],
 "Pairing relaxation with steps of a fear hierarchy, from least to most feared, counters the anxiety response through reciprocal inhibition."),
("A solution-focused counselor asks, \"What will be the first small sign tomorrow that things are better?\" This aims to:",
 "Make the goal concrete", ["Uncover past trauma", "Analyze the transference", "Confront her denial"],
 "Solution-focused questions about small, observable signs of change turn a vague hope into a concrete goal the client can notice and build on."),
("A client says, \"The anger takes over and ruins my weekends.\" A narrative counselor would most likely ask:",
 "\"When have you beaten the anger?\"", ["\"Why are you an angry person?\"", "\"What did your father do wrong?\"", "\"How strong is your anger, 1-10?\""],
 "Narrative work separates the person from the problem and looks for times the client resisted it, which opens an alternative story."),
("A Gestalt counselor notices a client's fist clench while talking calmly about her boss. The counselor might say:",
 "\"What is your fist saying?\"", ["\"You must be very angry.\"", "\"Let's talk about something else.\"", "\"Your boss sounds unfair.\""],
 "Gestalt therapy brings attention to present-moment awareness and the body. Inviting the client to give the fist a voice brings out feeling she has not put into words."),
("A reality therapy counselor asks a client what he wants and whether his current behavior is getting it. This targets:",
 "Self-evaluation", ["Early recollections", "Unconscious conflict", "Classical conditioning"],
 "Reality therapy, built on choice theory, asks clients to evaluate whether what they are doing helps them meet their needs, then plan a change."),
("An Adlerian counselor asks a shy client to act for one week as if she were confident. This technique is:",
 "Acting as if", ["Spitting in the soup", "Push button", "Empty chair"],
 "In acting as if, the client tries on a desired way of being. Spitting in the soup exposes the hidden payoff of a behavior; push button links thoughts to feelings."),
("A client says he keeps \"helping\" his sister so she will owe him. An Adlerian counselor names this payoff aloud so it loses its appeal. This is:",
 "Spitting in the soup", ["Acting as if", "Catching oneself", "Task setting"],
 "Spitting in the soup points out the hidden purpose of a behavior so the client cannot keep doing it as innocently as before."),
("A client grieving a lost career asks what her life means now. An existential counselor would help her:",
 "Find meaning she chooses", ["Correct distorted thoughts", "Learn new coping drills daily", "Recall early memories"],
 "Existential counseling, including Frankl's logotherapy, holds that people can find meaning even in suffering, through what they choose to value and do."),
("A client with insomnia lies awake trying hard to sleep. The counselor tells him to try to stay awake instead. This is:",
 "Paradoxical intention", ["Sleep restriction", "Thought stopping", "Flooding with imagery"],
 "Paradoxical intention, from Frankl, asks the client to try to do the feared thing, which reduces the performance anxiety that keeps the symptom going."),
("A person-centered counselor responds to a client's tears by saying, \"This loss still hurts deeply.\" The skill is:",
 "Reflecting feeling", ["Interpreting the tears", "Giving advice", "Probing for facts"],
 "Reflecting feeling names the emotion the client is expressing. Rogers held that empathy, genuineness and positive regard are the core conditions for change."),
("A client with alcohol use disorder says, \"I guess my drinking has cost me my license.\" A motivational interviewing response is:",
 "\"It's cost you a lot.\"", ["\"You need to quit now.\"", "\"Have you tried AA yet?\"", "\"Why did you drive drunk?\""],
 "Reflecting the client's own concern reinforces change talk. Directives and \"why\" questions tend to raise defensiveness."),
("A client facing a painful decision lists pros and cons of change and of staying the same. This MI tool is a:",
 "Decisional balance", ["Behavior chain analysis", "Fear hierarchy", "Family genogram"],
 "A decisional balance helps clients look at both sides of ambivalence. It is used with care, since it can also strengthen reasons not to change."),
("A client says, \"I'm worthless,\" after a breakup. The counselor replies, \"Part of you feels unlovable right now.\" This response:",
 "Reflects, not agrees", ["Confirms the belief", "Gives her advice", "Changes the topic to work"],
 "Reflecting the feeling and the timing (\"right now\") shows empathy without endorsing the belief that she is worthless."),
("A client arrives in a panic attack, breathing fast. The counselor should first:",
 "Coach slow breathing", ["Explore its childhood roots", "Assign homework", "Interpret the attack"],
 "During acute panic, calming the body with paced breathing and grounding comes first. Exploring causes comes once the client is calm."),
("A client who dissociates when recalling abuse begins to stare blankly. The counselor should:",
 "Use grounding", ["Push for more detail", "End the session at once", "Interpret the silence"],
 "Grounding, such as noticing five things she can see, brings the client back to the present before trauma work continues."),
("A client describes a fight with her partner and says, \"He never listens.\" The counselor asks, \"What did he say?\" This uses:",
 "A concrete probe", ["Self-disclosure", "Reframing her view", "Immediacy"],
 "Asking for specific details turns a general complaint into something workable. Vague statements such as \"never\" are often clarified this way."),
("A client who blames herself for her son's arrest is told, \"Your worry shows how much you care.\" This is:",
 "Reframing", ["Confrontation", "Interpretation", "Clarifying"],
 "Reframing offers a new, more useful meaning for the same facts. It does not deny the problem; it changes the client's view of it."),
("A client keeps asking her counselor what she should do about her marriage. The best response is to:",
 "Help her weigh options", ["Tell her to leave him", "Tell her to stay with him", "Share the counselor's story"],
 "Counselors support client autonomy and decision-making rather than imposing their own choice. Self-disclosure here would shift focus to the counselor."),
("A counselor considers telling a client about her own divorce. The key question to ask herself is:",
 "Will it serve the client?", ["Will the client like me?", "Is the story interesting?", "Is the event recent?"],
 "Self-disclosure is used only when it helps the client and is brief, then focus returns to the client."),
("A client says she is \"fine\" with her diagnosis but cancels every session after hearing it. The counselor gently notes the pattern. This is:",
 "Gentle confrontation", ["Reflection of content", "Paraphrasing her words", "Summarizing the session"],
 "Constructive confrontation points out discrepancies between words and actions in a caring way, so the client can look at them."),
("A client with an addiction misses many sessions and blames everyone. The counselor first works to:",
 "Strengthen the alliance", ["Assign blame to the client", "End services quickly instead", "Report him to the court"],
 "A strong working alliance is one of the best predictors of outcome. Engagement comes before pushing for change."),
("A veteran says he does not trust civilians who \"never served.\" The counselor should:",
 "Invite his view of the gap", ["Insist she understands him", "Turn the talk to his family", "Refer him elsewhere at once"],
 "Aligning the intervention with the client's population starts with honest acknowledgment and curiosity about his experience, which builds trust."),
("A counselor sees a client by video and the connection drops mid-session. Ideally this was planned for by:",
 "A backup phone plan", ["Ending the session early", "Calling the police at once", "Billing it as a no-show"],
 "Telemental health practice includes a plan for technology failure, such as a phone number to call, agreed at the start."),
("A counselor gives a client with anxiety a handout on the fight-or-flight response. This is:",
 "Psychoeducation", ["Interpretation", "Free association", "Desensitization"],
 "Psychoeducation teaches the client about symptoms and how treatment works, which reduces fear of the symptoms themselves."),
("A client says, \"You remind me of my critical father.\" The counselor uses this to explore:",
 "Old relationship patterns", ["Her own childhood losses", "The fee schedule", "Medication options"],
 "Educating the client about transference and exploring it helps him see how old relationship patterns shape present ones."),
("A teen with ADHD loses points for each missed chore and earns them for each finished one. This approach uses:",
 "A token economy", ["Systematic desensitization", "Free association", "Paradoxical intention"],
 "A token economy reinforces target behaviors with points or tokens exchanged for rewards. Losing tokens for problem behavior is response cost."),
]
